window.MANUAL_INDEX = [{"url": "getting-started.html", "title": "Getting started", "summary": "Signing in, creating an account and finding your way around Cupcake Vanilla.", "text": "Cupcake Vanilla keeps the sample metadata of proteomics experiments in tables that follow the SDRF format, and exports them as SDRF or Excel files. Signing in Sign in with your username and password, or with Login with ORCID when the site allows it. Tick Keep me logged in on this device to stay signed in. Signing in New users choose Create Account when the site allows public registration; otherwise an administrator creates the account. Metadata Management After signing in, Cupcake Vanilla opens on Metadata Management: Metadata Tables lists your tables and creates new ones; see Metadata tables . Metadata Templates keeps reusable column and table templates; see Templates . Quick Actions creates a template or a table straight away. Metadata management The navigation bar leads to the tables, templates, vocabularies, favourites and lab groups, and the account menu to your profile, device tokens and, for staff, the administration pages."}, {"url": "metadata-tables.html", "title": "Metadata tables", "summary": "Finding, creating, validating, exporting and deleting metadata tables.", "text": "Metadata Tables lists the tables you can open: your own, those of your lab groups and, with Show Shared Tables , those shared with you. Metadata tables Finding a table Search & Filters narrows the list by name, lab group, status (published or draft) and locked tables. Column Content finds tables by a column name or value, of any type or only characteristics, factor values or comments; Exact match matches the whole value. Staff see every table with Admin View (All Tables) . Creating a table Choose New Table . The list of metadata tables, with New Table at the top Enter the table's name and its number of samples, from 1 to 10,000. Optionally describe it, give it a version and assign it to a lab group; a table without a lab group is personal. Naming the new table and giving its number of samples Save. The table appears in the list. The new table in the list Use Template creates a table from a table template instead, with its columns already set up; see Templates . Working with tables Each row's actions View , Edit , Validate , Export and Delete the table. Locked tables are marked and cannot be changed. Select several tables to export them together with Bulk Export SDRF or Bulk Export Excel ."}, {"url": "table-editor.html", "title": "Editing a table", "summary": "Columns, values, auto-fill, search and replace, and sample pools in a metadata table.", "text": "Opening a table shows its information (sample count, columns, owner, lab group, version, and whether it is locked or published) and its contents, as a List of columns or as a Table of samples by columns. The new table, ready for its samples Editing a metadata table Columns Add Column adds a column from a column template; columns follow the order of the table's SDRF schemas, and Reorder restores that order. Drag a column to move it. Each column is marked when it is required, read-only, hidden or filled automatically. A column's actions: Edit default value : the value of every sample that has no value of its own. Auto-fill values : fill samples from a template such as sample {n} or a numeric range ( Basic ), or generate combinations of several columns' values for many samples ( Advanced ). Edit column settings : its template, type, ontology, and whether it is mandatory, hidden, read-only or staff only. View column history : every change, with its old and new values. Filter columns shows only the columns whose names match. Values Click a cell to edit its value. The editor suggests terms from the column's ontology as you type, offers your favourite values and those of your lab group, and has quick choices such as Not Applicable and Not Available . Columns such as modifications, cleavage agents, ages, spiked compounds and m/z ranges have a structured editor that writes the SDRF value for you; turn it off to type the value directly. Apply to Samples writes the value to several samples at once, and Add to Favorites keeps it for later; see Favourites . Search and Replace replaces every cell holding exactly one value, in one column or all of them, and optionally in the sample pools. It cannot be undone. Sample pools Create Pool groups samples into a pool: choose the samples, or a range, and whether each is only pooled or also run on its own. A reference pool is marked as such. Pools are exported with the table. The table itself Edit changes the table's name, description, sample count, version and lab group. Delete Table removes it."}, {"url": "import-export.html", "title": "Importing, exporting and validating", "summary": "Filling a table from SDRF, Excel or CSV files, exporting it, and checking it against the SDRF format.", "text": "Importing Import on a table offers: Import SDRF File : reads the samples and columns of an SDRF file into the table. Override count uses the file's number of samples instead of the table's, and Apply schema adds the columns of the SDRF schema the file declares. Import Excel Template : reads a table exported with Export as Excel and filled in. Import / Override Columns : maps the columns of an SDRF, Excel or CSV file onto the table's columns, by name, by position or by hand, shows every change before it is made, and creates the columns the table does not have. To fill a new table from an SDRF file: Open the table and choose Import , then Import SDRF File . Choosing Import SDRF File from the Import menu Choose the file and confirm. Confirming the import of the SDRF file into the table The table now holds the file's samples and columns. The samples and columns read from the SDRF file Exporting Export writes the table as an SDRF file, or as an Excel workbook that can be filled in and imported again. The Excel export can include the value recommendations of your lab groups and a sheet for the sample pools. Exporting the table as SDRF or Excel Open in Excel gives a code that the Cupcake Vanilla add-in for Excel opens the table with: in Excel, open the add-in, choose Use Launch Code and enter the code. Validating Validate checks a table against the SDRF format and the schemas you choose: the required columns, the column headers and the values. Skip Ontology Validation is faster but does not look up ontology terms, and Include Sample Pools checks the pools too. Errors must be fixed before the table is valid SDRF; warnings point at possible problems."}, {"url": "sharing.html", "title": "Sharing a table", "summary": "Lab groups, share links, and locked and published tables.", "text": "A table assigned to a lab group is open to the group's members. To show a table to someone without an account, open it and choose Share , then Generate Share Link : anyone with the link can view the table without signing in. Copy link copies the link. Regenerate replaces the link; the old one stops working. Revoke removes the link. A locked table cannot be changed, and a published table is marked as final rather than a draft."}, {"url": "templates.html", "title": "Templates", "summary": "Column templates and table templates, and creating tables from them.", "text": "Column templates A column template defines a column: its SDRF column name, such as characteristics[organism] , its type, its ontology for suggestions, a default value, and who can use it (only you, a lab group, or everyone). Built-in templates come from the SDRF schemas; you can add your own. Column templates Column Templates searches templates by name, schema, lab group and visibility. The grouped view gathers the templates of the same column name. New Template creates one; each template's actions edit, duplicate or delete it. Table templates A table template is a set of columns to start tables from. Table templates Create from Schemas builds a template from SDRF schemas: a technology schema as the base, with sample or organism and experiment schemas added as needed. Manual Configuration starts an empty template that you add columns to. A template can belong to a lab group, and can be the default template for new tables. Its actions create a table from it, edit, duplicate or delete it, reorder its columns by schema, and add the columns its schemas have gained since. To create a table from a template, choose Create metadata table from this template , then enter the table's name and number of samples."}, {"url": "vocabularies.html", "title": "Vocabularies", "summary": "The term lists that columns suggest values from, and adding your own.", "text": "A vocabulary is a list of terms that a column suggests values from, such as organisms, diseases or instruments. Vocabularies lists them with who they are available to, their number of terms, their status and when they were last loaded. Vocabularies provided by a plugin wait for approval by staff before they can be used. The vocabularies available for metadata columns Adding a vocabulary Choose New vocabulary , then enter the kind of term, its SDRF short name, a description and a label; the key is made from the label. Describing a new custom vocabulary Create it. It has no terms yet. The new vocabulary in the list, still without terms Choose Import terms and pick an OBO, OWL, CSV or TSV file. For CSV and TSV files, choose which columns hold the names, ids, descriptions and synonyms. Add and update terms keeps the terms already there; Replace all terms replaces them. Choosing which file columns hold the names, ids and synonyms The vocabulary now has its terms. The vocabulary after its terms were imported Browse terms searches the terms, edits them, adds new ones and marks obsolete ones. Browsing and editing the terms of the vocabulary Delete removes a vocabulary that no column uses. Deleting a vocabulary that no column uses"}, {"url": "favourites.html", "title": "Favourites", "summary": "Keeping values you use often, for yourself, your lab group or everyone.", "text": "A favourite is a value kept for a column, offered first when you edit that column's values. A favourite is personal , shared with a lab group , or global , for everyone. Favourite values Favorite Management searches favourites by name or value, scope, lab group and column type. Add Favorite adds one: the column name, such as characteristics[organism] , the value, an optional display name, and its scope. Values are also added from the value editor with Add to Favorites ."}, {"url": "lab-groups.html", "title": "Lab groups", "summary": "Creating lab groups, inviting members and sharing tables with them.", "text": "A lab group shares tables, templates and favourites among its members. Lab groups New Lab Group creates a group; Allow members to invite others lets every member invite. Invite Member sends an invitation by email address, with an optional message. Invitations to you appear at the top, to Accept or Reject . View members lists the members; those who manage the group can remove members and cancel invitations. Leave group removes you from a group you did not create, and Delete group removes a group you manage."}, {"url": "profile-and-devices.html", "title": "Your profile and device tokens", "summary": "Your name, password, email address and appearance, and tokens for devices.", "text": "Profile User Profile shows your account and changes your name, password and email address; each change asks for your current password, and a new email address is confirmed by email. Your profile Appearance chooses the theme: Default , E-Ink for e-ink displays, or Stylized Sci-Fi Compact . Light or dark mode is switched in the navigation bar. Device tokens A device token lets a device, such as a lab display, use Cupcake Vanilla as you without your password. Device tokens New Token creates one with a label, a description, read-only or read and write access, and an optional expiry date. Copy the token when it is shown: it is not shown again. Rotate token replaces a token, and Delete removes it."}, {"url": "administration.html", "title": "Administration", "summary": "Site settings and user accounts, for staff.", "text": "Site settings Site Configuration sets the site's name, logo and main colour, and whether \"Powered by CUPCAKE Vanilla\" is shown. Under Authentication Settings it turns public registration and ORCID sign-in on or off. Audio/Video Transcription chooses the Whisper model used to transcribe recordings and shows the background workers. Site settings Users User Management searches accounts by name, staff status and account status, and creates, edits and deletes them. Reset password sets a new password, optionally asking the user to change it at the next sign-in. Managing users"}, {"url": "appliance.html", "title": "Running the appliance", "summary": "Storage, backups, Wi-Fi and power on a Cupcake appliance.", "text": "Storage Storage Management mounts a USB drive by its label, or an NFS or SMB share, at /mnt/cupcake-data , and shows what is mounted. Backups Backup Management backs up the database, the uploaded files, or both, to the mounted storage or another folder under /mnt/cupcake-data or /opt/cupcake/backups , and lists the backups made. Wi-Fi WiFi Management connects to a Wi-Fi network with a password (WPA2 Personal) or an institutional account (WPA2 Enterprise, such as eduroam), and shows the connection. Power Power restarts the backend or the appliance, or shuts it down. After shutting down, wait until its lights stop before unplugging it."}, {"url": "plugins.html", "title": "Plugins", "summary": "Pages and vocabularies added by plugins.", "text": "Plugins add pages and vocabularies to Cupcake Vanilla. Their pages appear in the navigation once the plugin is installed; their vocabularies wait for approval by staff before columns can use them. Plugins are installed by an administrator."}];
